WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.850
Climate change has been
considered a middle class issue.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.920
When a community is
faced in survival mode,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.920
there tends to be a gap as far
as what you can actually do.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.870
These are the type of neighborhoods

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.700
that deal with higher asthma rates

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.080
and are considered front line communities,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.110
They're closer to the incinerators.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.790
They're closer to the railroad tracks.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.763
However, they're left out of clean energy.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.300
Kristal Hansley is trying
to expand the reach

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.730
of renewable energy,

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.960
and address systemic
imbalances in the process.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.100
She's the first Black
woman to run a company

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.540
specializing in community solar -

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.540
a fast-growing part of the industry

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.210
that she hopes will make it easier

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.650
for marginalized communities to go green,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.570
and see economic benefits in the process.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.540
My vision is to bring solar
to every city in the country,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.570
and so cities like Baltimore City

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.020
will be able to be a part
of that just transition

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.203
across the nation.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.710
Solar is going to be fairly critical

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.040
to the overall decarbonization goal.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.010
It's one of the cheapest
forms of clean energy around.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.913
And costs are steadily declining.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.020
To really get a sense of
what how far solar has come

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.220
need to look at over the
last sort of 10 years.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.870
In the early 2010 or
so, solar electricity,

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.700
it would cost you about
$300 per megawatt hour.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.890
Today, you can get that electricity

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.273
for around $30 a megawatt hour.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.160
And what has really driven that

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.760
is a sort of fall in the
cost of the solar panels.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.463
Manufacturing efficiency
has steadily improved

00:01:52.463 --> 00:01:56.600
as well as well as the
efficiency of a panel.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.520
Those cost declines have turned solar

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.340
into a booming industry in recent years.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.860
In the U.S., not even a
pandemic could stop 2020

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.683
from becoming a record
year for new installations.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.940
But those gains haven't
been evenly distributed.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.380
Traditionally, there've been barriers

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.350
that precluded
African-American communities,

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.170
underrepresented communities
from accessing solar

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.500
for a simple fact that
you needed to own a home

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.340
or have 700 credit score
to get a nice interest rate

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.120
on a solar loan.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.280
As a result, many minorities
are left out of the savings

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.680
that can come with switching to solar.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.610
Now, a new type of solar business

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.850
is looking to solve that problem.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:40.050
Community solar is a model

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:44.020
in which anyone who might
consider putting solar

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.780
on their roof but is not able to do so

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.520
for whatever purposes,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.670
signs up to receive a
share of the electricity

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.460
from a larger project.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.050
So, community solar is an approach

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:00.173
that lets everybody participate
in the benefits of solar.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.590
Tom Hunt's company, Pivot Energy,

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.660
is based in Denver, Colorado,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.030
and has deployed around
50 megawatts of solar

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.530
across the country.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:10.363
You know, that might serve

00:03:10.363 --> 00:03:13.510
something between 5,000
and 10,000 customers total.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.690
He estimates at least half their business

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.550
is in community solar.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.460
It's growing very fast for us.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.140
I would guess in 2021, we might deploy

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.590
double the amount of community solar

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:24.993
that we deployed in 2020.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.530
Community solar started right
here in Colorado, actually

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.170
back in about 2010 or so.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.980
Colorado is a great place, obviously,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.030
to develop solar, you have
lots of sunshine here.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.510
But even more so, you have
a really strong foundation

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.230
of experts in the technology

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.190
and the commercialization of solar.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.830
A lot of that, I think, comes from having

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.460
the National Renewable
Energy Laboratory here,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.010
there out in Golden and have
been around for decades.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.140
And so, I think that set
a really strong foundation

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.173
to have the solar industry grow here

00:03:58.173 --> 00:04:00.163
and make impact on the world.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:03.660
We are at the site

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.900
of one of Pivot's community solar gardens

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.203
on the outskirts of the Denver metro area.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.950
The subscribers are a nice
mix of local governments,

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.077
juts regular individuals
that want to participate

00:04:15.077 --> 00:04:18.083
and a few organizations that
serve low-income community.

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:20.800
Obviously, we produce clean energy,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.420
but with community solar,
we're also engaging

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.870
a wide set of the community

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.933
in participating in that solution.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.860
From its origins in Colorado,

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.440
community solar started
picking up steam in 2016,

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.640
and it's since spread
to nearly every state.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.783
One up-and-coming hotspot is
the greater Baltimore area.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.780
This farm on the city's
outskirts, built in 2020,

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.280
was developed by Bithenergy,
whose CEO, Robert Wallace,

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.157
is one of the few Black business owners

00:04:53.157 --> 00:04:54.980
in the solar industry.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.250
Baltimore, like every
other city, in my opinion,

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.890
need solar energy.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.910
It reduces the carbon footprint.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.210
But it also is an economic engine.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.850
Our process involves using local minority

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.970
and women businesses to
help build the projects.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.550
Baltimore's about two
thirds African-American.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:14.090
I believe that solar energy,
like every other industry,

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:17.500
should reflect the communities
in which it serves.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.080
And that is a smart business principle.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.860
I think it's the right thing to do

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.233
and the moral thing to do.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.220
Wallace says his company has
three community solar farms

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.310
currently serving the Baltimore area,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.203
with another seven in the works.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.960
Meanwhile, Kristal Hansley of WeSolar

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.710
is working to make sure
Baltimore's poorest residents

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.690
can reap the benefits of community solar.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:42.970
State subsidies allow low-income residents

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.690
to save up to 25% on their electric bill

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.290
if they make the switch.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.870
So that's really huge for
folks who are still, you know,

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.540
struggling while we climb
our way out of this pandemic.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.220
But it's often easier said than done.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.590
The main challenges that's
associated with my role

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.880
in the industry, which is
managing a subscribing customers

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.563
for these type of solar
projects, is the trust.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.530
Folks typically do not believe

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.820
that those savings that
you're promising will exist,

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.530
It's new, no one's seen it before,

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.700
coupled with they've been bamboozled

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.123
and lied to in the past.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.360
To overcome those
barriers, Kristal partners

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.960
with local community leaders,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.960
such as Reverend Cleveland Mason,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.500
who opened his church to Kristal

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.989
to make her pitch to his congregation.

00:06:30.989 --> 00:06:32.120
How you doing, Rev?

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.460
Doing great.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.580
I'm always looking for something

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.220
that's gonna benefit our people.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:38.820
As long as we can help our people

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:42.210
understand benefits like this
that are available to them,

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.120
we're helping them to save money.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.320
We're helping them to make
their life a little bit easier.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.430
But COVID has made those outreach efforts

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.110
more challenging,
especially since low-income

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:54.980
and elderly residents are less likely

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.920
to have internet access.

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:58.660
You know, I'm still bringing people online

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.280
for worship services.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:01.340
But it's a process.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:06.340
You know, these people are
just new to the technology.

00:07:07.148 --> 00:07:09.910
Most of them are not
doing something different

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.570
simply because they just don't know.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.170
And I think it's our job to help make sure

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.860
that they are aware and, hopefully,

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.453
improving their lifestyle along the way.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:25.730
Ultimately, community
solar is just one part

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:29.070
of the remarkable success
story of the solar industry,

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.260
which has become an undeniable bright spot

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.930
in the fight against climate change.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.820
But it isn't yet clear how big a part

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.563
this new business model will play.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:40.410
In terms of the scale

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.960
at which solar more broadly can grow,

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.720
a lot of the growth
rate is actually coming

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.843
from like utility scale power plants.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.930
In terms of adding more clean
energy to the grid, overall,

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.873
those projects are definitely doing more.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.990
But community solar can
definitely allow more companies,

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:05.990
more people to get access to
sort of the benefits of solar.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:10.990
You know, my my company has
begun doing projects globally.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.210
We just kicked off a project
in the Virgin Islands.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:17.030
We've we've done we've
marketed in West Africa

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.210
and Latin America.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.330
This is an emerging global market.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.350
We have a burgeoning middle class.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:25.130
I think that to the community solar model

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:29.110
is the perfect model to accelerate
the penetration of solar

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:30.363
in those markets.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:34.330
The solar industry is in
a really strong position.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:37.590
At this point, solar is
just about the cheapest

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.210
form of energy possible for electricity.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:41.690
For those of us who have
been in the industry

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:44.470
for quite a while, it's
something we knew we would get to

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.080
but had to fight quite
a bit to make it happen.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.900
I expect that by the end
of my working career,

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.140
solar will be the largest
source of electricity

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.463
in the United States.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:57.770
It's is cool and awesome

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.260
like to say that you have a company

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.750
that's not only fighting climate change,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.663
but also saving low income families money.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.930
My end goal is to be able to bring solar

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:14.150
to every single household in the country.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:15.100
That's my goal

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.903
and I think it's definitely
viable and feasible.

